{
  "issue": 99,
  "version": 2,
  "understanding": "Two separate things went wrong. FIRST, the plan reviewer is right and this revision corrects his objection: the previous work order asserted its new BUG-3 unit case 'fails today for exactly the reason QA reported', and it does not. It prescribed `runCheck(repo.dir, { GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY: <temp file> })`, but runCheck unconditionally injects `BASE_REF: BASE_BRANCH` into the child env, so that call leaves BASE_REF SET — and with BASE_REF set the unfixed script prints exactly one ::error:: line, no 'BASE_REF env var is not set' line, and exits 1, which is precisely what all three of the case's assertions require. I reproduced this directly: with the previous order's exact call, the case passes 84/84 against the UNFIXED script. The same plan contradicted itself — tests[] said 'BASE_REF unset' while files[] prescribed a call that leaves it set, and that contradiction is what produced the vacuous test. I fixed the harness as the reviewer prescribed (an env-override parameter that can DELETE a variable, merged after the BASE_REF injection, dropping undefined-valued keys rather than stringifying them — I verified Node's execFile omits undefined-valued env keys entirely) and re-ran it: with `BASE_REF: undefined` the case fails against d4684c80 and passes with the fix. AC-11's end-to-end command was already correct; the defect was confined to the unit case, as the reviewer said, and files[] now matches tests[].\n\nSECOND, and this is the diagnosis the previous attempt got wrong. It said its root cause was RIGHT because QA confirmed the headline fix real (all eight original criteria pass; a nested note now reaches the changelog and the generated docs page). That much is true. But the previous attempt carried a premise it never examined: it treated 'the two sides must agree on WHICH files exist' as the whole invariant, and asserted a nested README.md being validated as a note is 'the correct outcome of that shared rule, not a bug to route around'. Unifying the enumeration was necessary but not sufficient. The gate and the publisher now agree on the file SET and disagree on what happens when a member of that set is malformed. The gate (validateNote) turns every malformation into a named failure string and keeps going; the publisher (parseReleaseNotes) dereferences data.authors unguarded and dies. That asymmetry was harmless while the publisher only read top-level files the gate had already cleared — an invariant the recursive walk breaks, because gate and publisher now agree a NESTED file is a note and the publisher has no validateNote equivalent. So the root cause is a behaviour-parity gap, not an enumeration gap. I reproduced all three bugs against d4684c80 myself in a worktree at that commit: BUG-1 parseReleaseNotes throws 'TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading map)' on any note with no authors list; BUG-2 collectFailures wraps the ENTIRE recursive walk in one try/catch, so one chmod-000 subdirectory discards every failure already accumulated; BUG-3 the new guard in release-notes-check.mjs calls reportError from module scope, but reportError only exits synchronously when GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY is ABSENT — the one environment a real Actions runner never has and the one the unit harness deletes. All 83 existing tests pass on that commit; nothing here is a red suite, all three bugs sit outside coverage.\n\nOne further finding the previous attempt did not have: its BUG-2 fix text said '`path` is already imported at the top of the file', but following that literally (`join(dir, relative)`) crashes with 'ReferenceError: join is not defined' — I hit exactly that while prototyping, because release-notes-gate.mjs imports `* as path from 'node:path'` and has no bare `join` binding. The change text below names `path.join` explicitly.",
  "root_cause": "The shared walk made the gate and the publisher agree on WHICH files are release notes, but nothing made them agree on WHAT a malformed member of that set does. release-notes-gate.mjs's validateNote turns every rule violation into a named failure string and keeps going; parseReleaseNotes has no equivalent and dereferences data.authors.map with no Array.isArray guard. Before this PR that was latent, because the publisher only ever read top-level files the gate had already cleared. The recursive walk routed nested files into it, so the pre-existing unguarded dereference became reachable. Two smaller instances of the same 'report, do not abort and do not discard' rule were broken by this PR's own additions: collectFailures' single try/catch around the whole walk converts a partial walk into a total one (a throw at depth N discards depths 0..N-1), and reportError defers process.exit to a WriteStream 'close' event whenever GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY is set, so it is not a terminating function on the only platform it runs on.",
  "approach": "Three targeted repairs, each making one participant obey the rule the other two already follow. Nothing about the enumeration changes and no acceptance criterion is rewritten — all eight original criteria pass today and stay passing (verified: 91/91 green with all three prototypes applied).\n\nBUG-1: guard the dereference in parseReleaseNotes with Array.isArray(data.authors), and on failure THROW an Error naming the note's path relative to the notes directory. Throwing rather than skipping is load-bearing: release-notes-generate.mjs:264 unlinks every entry of the `files` array, and `files` comes from listNotePaths regardless of whether the note parsed — a skipped note would be deleted unpublished, the exact 'validated, counted, then published nowhere and credited to nobody' defect this ticket exists to close, one layer down.\n\nBUG-2: move the try/catch INSIDE the walk via an optional onError callback on listNotePaths; with no callback the walk rethrows exactly as today. collectFailures passes a callback accumulating a 'cannot read <path>: <message>' per unreadable directory and returns those alongside, not instead of, the per-note failures. The top-level directory still yields a single string (empty prefix), which keeps AC-3 true. IMPORTANT: in release-notes-gate.mjs write `path.join(dir, relative)`, not a bare `join(...)` — that file imports `* as path` and has no bare `join` binding.\n\nBUG-3: make reportError terminating on both paths by replacing the createWriteStream(...).on('close', exit) tail with a synchronous fs.appendFileSync guarded by try/catch, followed by an unconditional process.exit(1). appendFileSync is why this works: nothing needs flushing before the process leaves. The try/catch is load-bearing — appendFileSync throws on an unwritable summary path, which would replace today's crash with a different crash at the same exit code.\n\nThe harness is what let BUG-3 hide, and this is the part the plan reviewer rejected. runCheck gains an optional second parameter for env overrides, applied AFTER `const env = { ...process.env, BASE_REF: BASE_BRANCH }`, treating an `undefined` value as a DELETE rather than stringifying it. The new BUG-3 case MUST pass `BASE_REF: undefined`; without it the case passes against the unfixed script and proves nothing.\n\nEvery new case is falsifiability-checked by reverting its source fix and recording the failure, not merely recorded green.",
  "files": [
    {
      "path": "packages/ci-actions/src/release-notes/util.mjs",
      "action": "edit",
      "change": "In parseReleaseNotes, immediately after `const { data, content: body } = matter(content);` and before the `data.authors.map` call, insert a guard: `if (!Array.isArray(data.authors)) throw new Error(...)` whose message names the note by its path relative to the notes directory (`name` is already in scope), states that it has no usable `authors` list, and says the gate rejects such a note so reaching the publisher means the note was written after the gate ran. Mirrors release-notes-gate.mjs's `!Array.isArray(data.authors)` branch. Do NOT skip the file instead of throwing — release-notes-generate.mjs unlinks every path in the returned `files` array, so a skipped note would be deleted unpublished. One guard covers both the missing key and a bare-string `authors`, since neither is an array.\n\nIn listNotePaths, add a second optional parameter `onError`. Move the directory read inside the recursion into a try/catch: on failure, `if (onError) { onError(prefix, error); return; }` and otherwise `throw error` — so a caller passing no callback keeps today's behaviour exactly. Keep the rest of the walk, including the sort and its oxlint-disable comment, untouched. Extend the doc comment to say the callback exists so one unreadable subdirectory cannot discard the notes already walked, and that omitting it keeps the throwing behaviour parseReleaseNotes relies on.",
      "why": "BUG-1's crash and BUG-2's discarded failures both live in this file. BUG-1 is the only defect whose blast radius is an aborted release."
    },
    {
      "path": "packages/ci-actions/bin/release-notes-gate.mjs",
      "action": "edit",
      "change": "In the exported listNotePaths wrapper, forward the new parameter: `export function listNotePaths(dir = NOTES_DIR, onError) { return walkNotePaths(dir, onError); }`.\n\nRewrite collectFailures to stop wrapping the whole walk. Declare `const unreadable = []`, call `listNotePaths(dir, (relative, error) => { unreadable.push(`cannot read ${relative ? path.join(dir, relative) : dir}: ${error.message}`); })`, then return `[...unreadable, ...paths.flatMap(relativePath => validateNote(relativePath, dir))]`. Use `path.join`, NOT a bare `join(...)`: this file imports `* as path from 'node:path'` and has no bare `join` binding — writing `join(dir, relative)` here is a ReferenceError at runtime, which I hit while prototyping this exact fix. For a missing or unreadable TOP-LEVEL directory the prefix is empty, so the ternary yields `dir` itself and the result is the single 'cannot read <dir>: ...' string AC-3 already asserts; for a subdirectory it yields the subdirectory path, and the notes outside it are still validated. Update collectFailures' doc comment to state that an unreadable directory is reported alongside the notes that were read, never instead of them. Leave validateNote and main untouched.",
      "why": "Closes BUG-2 while leaving the gate's public surface and AC-3's single-line shape exactly as they are. Both properties verified against a prototype on the real module."
    },
    {
      "path": "packages/ci-actions/bin/release-notes-check.mjs",
      "action": "edit",
      "change": "In reportError, replace the tail that reads the `if (!process.env.GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY) { process.exit(1); }` early return followed by `fs.createWriteStream(...).end(readme).on('close', () => { process.exit(1); })` with: a `try { fs.appendFileSync(process.env.GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY, readme); } catch { /* degrade to no notice */ }` guarded by `if (process.env.GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY)`, then an unconditional `process.exit(1)`. The try/catch is required because appendFileSync THROWS on an unwritable summary path (QA's T-16 captured today's crash there, an unhandled WriteStream 'error' event), and without the guard the fix would replace one crash with another. Rewrite the comment above it, which currently claims the runner always supplies GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY and that without it there is nothing left to do but exit — after this change both branches do the same thing. No other change: the module-scope ENOENT guard at lines 20-28 needs no edit once reportError actually terminates.",
      "why": "BUG-3. The guard is correct in intent and wrong in mechanism: it called a function that returns instead of exiting on every real Actions run, so the BASE_REF check below it kept executing and printed a second ::error:: line."
    },
    {
      "path": "packages/ci-actions/src/release-notes/util.test.js",
      "action": "edit",
      "change": "Add a describe block covering the publisher's malformed-input path, which had no coverage at all — that is why BUG-1 reached a PR. Use fs.mkdtempSync under os.tmpdir() (never inside upcoming-release-notes/, per AC-5) and mock console.log the way the existing parseReleaseNotes block does, because resolvePrNumber shells out to git on a path outside any repository. Add: a case over a fixture holding sub/notes.md with NO front matter beside a valid top-level note, asserting the returned promise REJECTS and the rejection message names 'sub/notes.md' (assert on the path substring, never the full sentence); a case for `authors: Shivam-Fl` as a bare string, rejecting the same way; and a control case asserting a note WITH an authors list still publishes into notesByCategory, so the guard cannot be satisfied by rejecting everything.\n\nAdd a second describe block for the onError callback: build a fixture with a valid top-level note, a readable sub/ holding a valid note, and a subdirectory whose permissions are removed with fs.chmodSync(dir, 0o000). Pass a callback recording its (relative, error.code) arguments. Assert the callback fired once with relative === 'locked' and code 'EACCES', AND that the returned array still contains BOTH 'top.md' and 'readable/deep.md' — that second half is the regression BUG-2 was, and asserting only that the callback fires would let the old discarding behaviour pass. Add a case asserting that with NO callback a locked subdirectory still throws. Restore permissions in a finally so the tmpdir can be removed, and skip the whole block when `typeof process.getuid === 'function' && process.getuid() === 0` — root ignores permission bits, so the cases would record a vacuous pass. Verified: all three util.mjs cases and both onError cases fail against unfixed source and pass with the fix.",
      "why": "The publisher's malformed-input path had no coverage at all, which is why BUG-1 reached a PR. The onError case asserts the walk SURVIVES the error, not merely that it reports one."
    },
    {
      "path": "packages/ci-actions/bin/release-notes-check.test.mjs",
      "action": "edit",
      "change": "Give runCheck an optional second parameter for extra/overridden child env entries, merged over its existing env. The merge must happen AFTER `const env = { ...process.env, BASE_REF: BASE_BRANCH }` — that is what lets an override delete a variable the harness itself injects — and an entry whose value is `undefined` must DELETE that key rather than stringify it: iterate Object.entries and call `delete env[key]` for undefined, `env[key] = value` otherwise. Verified that Node's execFile omits undefined-valued env keys entirely. Leave the existing `delete env.GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY` in place so all thirteen pre-existing cases keep exercising the branch they were written against — do NOT flip the default, because that would silently change what six other assertions are testing.\n\nAdd the BUG-3 case: call setup(), remove path.join(repo.dir, NOTES_DIR) with fs.promises.rm recursive+force, write a summary path under repo.root, and call `runCheck(repo.dir, { BASE_REF: undefined, GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY: summary })`. The `BASE_REF: undefined` is load-bearing and must not be dropped: runCheck injects BASE_REF by default, and with it left set the unfixed script prints exactly one ::error:: line and exits 1, so the case passes vacuously. Confirmed — the previous revision prescribed the call without it and the case passed 84/84 against the unfixed script. Assert code 1, stdout contains '::error::Cannot read upcoming-release-notes/: ', and stdout does NOT contain 'BASE_REF env var is not set' — that last assertion is the visible proof that execution continued past the guard.\n\nAlso add a collectFailures case for BUG-2: a tmpdir holding one broken note (bad category plus a bot author) AND a chmod-000 subdirectory; assert collectFailures returns a 'cannot read ' entry AND two entries beginning 'zzz-broken.md', in one array. Assert on substrings, not whole strings. Add a further case asserting a top-level directory which does not exist still returns EXACTLY ONE string containing 'cannot read ' — AC-3's shape, which the fix must not widen. Restore permissions and remove the tmpdir in a finally; skip both under root (getuid() === 0).",
      "why": "BUG-3 is invisible to the suite precisely because runCheck deletes the variable that makes the new code misbehave, AND injects BASE_REF which hides the symptom. Without both halves the fix is unverified and the same defect returns."
    }
  ],
  "tests": [
    {
      "path": "packages/ci-actions/src/release-notes/util.test.js",
      "action": "edit",
      "cases": [
        "reproduces BUG-1: parseReleaseNotes rejects, rather than throwing 'Cannot read properties of undefined', when a nested note has no `authors` front matter, and the error names that note's relative path — VERIFIED to fail against unfixed util.mjs",
        "rejects a note whose `authors` is a bare string instead of a list, for the same reason and with the same named error — VERIFIED to fail against unfixed util.mjs",
        "still publishes a note that HAS an authors list, so the new guard is not satisfied by rejecting unconditionally",
        "reproduces BUG-2: listNotePaths' onError callback fires once naming the unreadable subdirectory ('locked') and its error code (EACCES), AND the returned array still holds both the top-level note and the readable nested note — VERIFIED to fail against unfixed util.mjs",
        "propagates the error when no onError callback is passed, preserving the throwing behaviour parseReleaseNotes relies on"
      ]
    },
    {
      "path": "packages/ci-actions/bin/release-notes-check.test.mjs",
      "action": "edit",
      "cases": [
        "reproduces BUG-3: release-notes-check.mjs run as a subprocess with GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY SET and BASE_REF DELETED (passed as `{ BASE_REF: undefined, GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY: <tmp> }` to the env-override parameter) emits '::error::Cannot read upcoming-release-notes/: ' and does NOT emit 'BASE_REF env var is not set', proving the guard halted the script — VERIFIED to fail against unfixed check.mjs; passing BASE_REF through as set instead makes it pass vacuously, the defect this revision corrects",
        "collectFailures reports an unreadable subdirectory AND both failures of the broken note outside it in a single array, rather than returning only the unreadable-directory message — VERIFIED to fail against unfixed gate.mjs",
        "collectFailures still returns exactly one 'cannot read ...' string naming the directory for a top-level directory that does not exist (AC-3's shape is unchanged)",
        "still passes every pre-existing case unchanged, including the thirteen subprocess cases whose runCheck keeps GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY unset and BASE_REF injected by default"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "acceptance": [
    {
      "id": "AC-1",
      "check": "A release note in a subdirectory of upcoming-release-notes/ reaches the published changelog: given a directory holding both top.md and sub/deep.md, parseReleaseNotes returns files containing 'sub/deep.md' and a notesByCategory entry whose text contains deep.md's body.",
      "how_to_verify": "Not a browser surface — release tooling with no screen. Run 'publishes a nested note's body and category into notesByCategory' in packages/ci-actions/src/release-notes/util.test.js.",
      "source": "TR-1",
      "verify": "test"
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-2",
      "check": "With no `only` argument, parseReleaseNotes enumerates exactly what listNotePaths returns for the same directory: given a tmpdir fixture holding top.md, sub/deep.md and sub/README.md plus a top-level README.md, the two agree element for element — both notes present, sub/README.md present, the top-level README.md absent from both.",
      "how_to_verify": "Run 'with no `only` argument, parseReleaseNotes enumerates exactly what listNotePaths returns for the same directory' in packages/ci-actions/src/release-notes/util.test.js. Scoped to the no-`only` case on purpose: with an `only` filter parseReleaseNotes returns a strict subset, which AC-8 asserts separately.",
      "source": "TR-2",
      "verify": "test"
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-3",
      "check": "Running the release-notes gate against a notes directory that does not exist prints a single line beginning '::error::cannot read ' and naming the directory, and exits 1. It does not print a Node stack trace. A subdirectory that cannot be read does not change this: only the top-level directory's own absence yields exactly one line.",
      "how_to_verify": "Run the collectFailures case 'still returns exactly one cannot read string naming the directory for a top-level directory that does not exist' in packages/ci-actions/bin/release-notes-check.test.mjs. For the process shape, run node packages/ci-actions/bin/release-notes-gate.mjs against a directory moved aside and expect exit 1 and one ::error:: line. QA confirmed this shape already holds at d4684c80; the fix must not widen it to a subdirectory.",
      "source": "TR-3",
      "verify": "test"
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-4",
      "check": "Running release-notes-check.mjs in a repository whose upcoming-release-notes/ does not exist prints a line beginning '::error::Cannot read upcoming-release-notes/: ' and exits 1, rather than a stack trace.",
      "how_to_verify": "Run 'drives release-notes-check.mjs as a subprocess against a repository whose upcoming-release-notes/ has been removed' in packages/ci-actions/bin/release-notes-check.test.mjs. Assert only the stable prefix; the ENOENT text after it varies by platform. Note that this criterion is about the message and exit code with GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY UNSET; AC-11 is the same guard under the runner's real environment, and the two must not be collapsed into one case.",
      "source": "TR-4",
      "verify": "test"
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-5",
      "check": "The @actual-app/ci-actions unit suite writes nothing inside upcoming-release-notes/: the nested-note case builds its fixture under the system temporary directory, and no qa-nested directory exists in the repository's notes directory afterwards, so a vitest process killed mid-run cannot leave a fixture that fails every later run.",
      "how_to_verify": "Run the full package suite (yarn workspace @actual-app/ci-actions run test) then `git status --porcelain -- upcoming-release-notes/` — expect empty output. The new chmod-000 and malformed-note fixtures in this attempt also live under os.tmpdir() and restore their permissions in a finally.",
      "source": "TR-5",
      "verify": "test"
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-6",
      "check": "release-notes-gate.mjs exports exactly listNotePaths and collectFailures; neither validateNote nor main carries the export keyword, and no dir parameter exists that no caller supplies.",
      "how_to_verify": "grep -n '^export' packages/ci-actions/bin/release-notes-gate.mjs — expect exactly two lines, listNotePaths and collectFailures. grep -rn \"from './release-notes-gate.mjs'\" packages/ — expect exactly one line, the import in release-notes-check.test.mjs. This attempt adds a second PARAMETER to the exported listNotePaths; it must not add a third export.",
      "source": "TR-6",
      "verify": "test"
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-7",
      "check": "The gate's failure report order is deterministic across runs and unaffected by the host locale: two notes failing at once always report in the same order, and that order is UTF-16 code-unit order, not locale-alphabetical.",
      "how_to_verify": "Run the sort-order case in util.test.js, then run the gate under LC_ALL=C and LC_ALL=en_US.UTF-8 against the same failing fixtures and confirm the ::error:: lines appear in the same order. The unreadable-directory entries collectFailures now prepends are emitted in walk order, which follows the recursive readdir and so is filesystem-dependent: if that ordering proves unstable across runs, sort the `unreadable` array before concatenating rather than leaving it to readdir order.",
      "source": "TR-7",
      "verify": "test"
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-8",
      "check": "The `only` filter matches the relative path, which is how release-notes-generate.mjs calls it: given a fixture holding top.md and sub/deep.md, only = ['sub/deep.md'] selects the nested note alone, and only = ['deep.md'] selects nothing.",
      "how_to_verify": "Run the two filter cases in packages/ci-actions/src/release-notes/util.test.js.",
      "source": "TR-8",
      "verify": "test"
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-9",
      "check": "A release note under upcoming-release-notes/ with no `authors` front-matter key — or with an `authors` value that is not a list — aborts the publish with an error naming that file by its path relative to the notes directory, instead of the publisher dying with 'Cannot read properties of undefined (reading \\'map\\')'. A note that DOES have an authors list still publishes normally. QA reproduced the bare TypeError against commit d4684c80; the same unguarded dereference existed at the top level before this PR, but the recursive walk made nested files reach it.",
      "how_to_verify": "Run the two BUG-1 cases in the malformed-note describe block in packages/ci-actions/src/release-notes/util.test.js, plus the control case that a valid note still publishes. End to end: write upcoming-release-notes/qa-nested/noauthors.md with no authors key, then run packages/docs/scripts/generate-upcoming-release-notes.mjs — expect a non-zero exit naming qa-nested/noauthors.md, not a TypeError, and NOT a silently omitted note. Confirm the case FAILS against d4684c80 before accepting it as a fix.",
      "source": "IAC-1",
      "verify": "test"
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-10",
      "check": "When one subdirectory of upcoming-release-notes/ cannot be read, the gate reports that directory AND every other note's failures in the same run. A contributor who fixes the unreadable directory and re-runs must not be meeting a second, previously hidden failure for the first time. Separately, a notes directory that does not exist AT ALL still produces exactly one failure string.",
      "how_to_verify": "Run 'collectFailures reports an unreadable subdirectory AND the notes outside it in a single array' and 'still returns exactly one cannot read string when the notes directory itself is missing' in packages/ci-actions/bin/release-notes-check.test.mjs. End to end: build a throwaway notes dir holding a note with a bad category and a bot author plus a chmod-000 subdirectory, call collectFailures on it, and expect the 'cannot read' entry AND the note's two failures in one array — at d4684c80 only the unreadable-directory message comes back. Note both cases must SKIP under root, where permission bits are ignored.",
      "source": "IAC-2",
      "verify": "test"
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-11",
      "check": "With GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY set — which is every GitHub Actions run, and the environment the current unit harness deletes — release-notes-check.mjs STOPS at its missing-notes-directory guard: exactly one '::error::Cannot read upcoming-release-notes/: ' line, no subsequent '::error::BASE_REF env var is not set' line, and exit 1. At d4684c80 the guard prints its error and execution continues into the BASE_REF check.",
      "how_to_verify": "Run 'reproduces BUG-3: halts at the missing-notes-directory guard with GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY set, as a runner has it' in packages/ci-actions/bin/release-notes-check.test.mjs. The case MUST pass `BASE_REF: undefined` through runCheck's env-override parameter so the child has no BASE_REF at all — runCheck injects BASE_REF by default, and with it left set the unfixed script prints one error line and the case passes vacuously, which is a defect this revision exists to correct. End to end, in a throwaway git repo with upcoming-release-notes/ removed: `GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY=/tmp/s.md env -u BASE_REF node packages/ci-actions/bin/release-notes-check.mjs` — expect exactly one ::error:: line and no BASE_REF line. Confirm the case FAILS against d4684c80 before accepting it. Both env shapes matter: AC-4 covers the variable unset, this one covers it set, and neither substitutes for the other.",
      "source": "IAC-3",
      "verify": "test"
    }
  ],
  "qa_script": [
    "yarn install — node_modules is absent in a fresh checkout and every command below fails for that reason alone",
    "yarn workspace @actual-app/ci-actions run test — expect 83 existing tests plus 8 new ones (91 total), all green. All 83 pass on d4684c80 today, so this is a floor, not the signal: the three bugs are outside existing coverage and only the NEW cases can catch them",
    "FALSIFIABILITY FIRST, before judging any fix: revert each source fix in turn and confirm the matching case fails. Against unfixed util.mjs + gate.mjs, three cases must fail (BUG-1 nested-no-authors, BUG-1 scalar-authors, BUG-2 onError); against unfixed check.mjs, the BUG-3 case must fail; against unfixed gate.mjs, the collectFailures BUG-2 case must fail. I ran all of these and they do fail. A green suite that never went red proves nothing about this change",
    "Confirm the BUG-3 harness correction is real, since it is the defect the plan reviewer caught: run the new case with `BASE_REF: undefined` (must fail against d4684c80), then change it to pass BASE_REF through as SET (must PASS against d4684c80 — a vacuous pass). If the second variant also fails, the harness is not reproducing what it claims",
    "Reproduce BUG-1 against the pre-fix commit before judging the fix: a tmpdir holding sub/notes.md with no front matter, then parseReleaseNotes over it — expect 'TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading map)' at d4684c80, and after the fix an error naming sub/notes.md",
    "Reproduce BUG-2: a tmpdir holding a note with category Nope and authors [github-actions], plus a chmod-000 subdirectory; call collectFailures — at d4684c80 expect ONLY the 'cannot read ... EACCES' string and none of the note's failures; after the fix expect both. chmod 755 the subdirectory as a control and confirm the note's two failures were always there underneath",
    "Reproduce BUG-3 in a throwaway git repo with upcoming-release-notes/ removed: GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY=<tmpfile> env -u BASE_REF node packages/ci-actions/bin/release-notes-check.mjs — at d4684c80 expect TWO ::error:: lines (the guard's, then 'BASE_REF env var is not set'); after the fix exactly one. Repeat with GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY unset to confirm AC-4's shape still holds",
